  "enhanced_information": "In the case of severe aplastic anemia, the standard therapeutic approach is determined by the availability of an HLA-matched sibling. If an HLA match is found, the preferred treatment is an allogeneic hematopoietic stem cell transplant, as it offers the best chance for a cure. This aligns with the consensus and is supported by evidence paths indicating the interaction of HLA antigens with T-lymphocytes, which is crucial for transplant compatibility. If no HLA match is available, the next best option is immunosuppressive therapy with cyclosporine and antithymocyte globulin, which is supported by evidence paths showing their role in therapeutic immunosuppression and interaction with T-lymphocytes. Supportive care, including blood and platelet transfusions, is used to manage symptoms temporarily but is not a definitive treatment. Options involving high-dose glucocorticoids or chemotherapy with autologous transplant are not standard treatments for severe aplastic anemia and do not align with consensus guidelines.",
  "analysis": "The standard treatment for severe aplastic anemia involves either hematopoietic stem cell transplantation or immunosuppressive therapy. In a 71-year-old patient, the first-line treatment typically involves immunosuppressive therapy due to the risks associated with transplantation in older patients. The use of cyclosporine and antithymocyte globulin (ATG) is a well-established approach for treating severe aplastic anemia. The retrieved knowledge supports the use of therapeutic immunosuppression for severe aplastic anemia, aligning with option opc.",
